  "title": "Databricks Genie Ontology Explained | Demo with Databricks Product Manager",
  "description": "How does \u202a@Databricks\u202c Genie Ontology connect Unity Catalog, business definitions, and AI agents? In this technical walkthrough, Databricks\u2019 Elise Georis joins Josue Bogran to explain modeled and learned context, OntoRank, and the sources Genie One uses to answer business questions.\n\nTopics include:\n-Modeled ontology: governed metric views, Pages, and domains.\n-Learned ontology: snippets extracted from existing assets and how teams use them.\n-OntoRank: certification, authorship, popularity, and source authority.\n-Text-to-SQL, Model Context Protocol (MCP) connectors, and search.\n-Source permissions, personal memory, citations, and tool governance.\n-Benchmark questions, domain experts, certified assets, and rollout evaluation.\n\nA Genie One demo shows the context behind a product-adoption analysis, including cited definitions and learned snippets, followed by forecasting, scheduled work, and creating a specialized Genie Agent.\n\nChapters\n00:00 Genie One, Genie Code and Genie Agents\n01:34 What is Databricks Genie Ontology?\n03:37 Unity Catalog: metric views, Pages and domains\n05:34 Learned ontology and knowledge snippets\n06:43 OntoRank: source authority and ranking\n07:33 Genie accuracy, latency and cost\n09:06 Text-to-SQL, MCP connectors and search\n11:21 Personal memory and user preferences\n12:51 Genie Ontology in Genie One demo: product-adoption analysis\n16:31 Citations, ontology snippets and authority scores\n18:15 Follow-up analysis, forecasting and skills\n20:22 Documents, MCP actions and governance\n24:23 Genie Agents, scheduling and integrations\n26:50 Demo: actions, schedules and shared agents\n29:49 Getting started: benchmarks and rollout\n32:37 Best practices: semantics, certification and access\n34:04 Closing",
